//
// WHAT IS WRAPPED. golf_clean.exe imports nothing from DirectPlay (import directory 0x004bf008, five DLLs: binkw32,
// Terrain, KERNEL32, USER32, WINMM), the string `DPLAYX.dll` at 0x004bf828 has no code reference, and its IDirectPlay2
// pointer 0x008400b0 is only read (Close through slot +0x10 at 0x00497d6a, Release through +0x8 at 0x00497d75) and cleared
// (0x00497d78). So the recorder does not depend on one creation path:
//   1. every export of the tables (DirectPlayCreate, DirectPlayLobbyCreateA/W, DirectPlayEnumerate(A/W), DllGetClassObject,
//      DllCanUnloadNow) is hooked in dplayx.dll as soon as it is mapped, whoever calls it;
//   2. golf_clean.exe's LoadLibraryA / GetProcAddress import slots (0x004ba0a8 / 0x004ba0a4) are wrapped and log every call
//      that names or returns a DirectPlay module (how the exe reached the entry point);
//   3. every interface pointer an export or a method returns through an out parameter (DirectPlayCreate's LPDIRECTPLAY *,
//      QueryInterface's / CreateInstance's / ConnectEx's LPVOID * typed by the REFIID before it, IDirectPlayLobby::Connect's
//      LPDIRECTPLAY2 *) gets its vtable slots replaced by per-(vtable, slot) thunks (8 nop + jmp [cell]) carrying an
//      Interceptor.attach, so a call is identified by its slot, never by the implementing function (dplayx can share bodies
//      between interfaces); slot counts and parameter kinds come from the generated NR_TABLES below (DirectX 8 SDK dplay.h /
//      dplobby.h, checked by tests/test_net_trace.py);
//   4. every callback the game passes (EnumSessions, EnumPlayers, EnumGroupPlayers, DirectPlayEnumerate, EnumConnections,
//      EnumAddress ...) is replaced in the argument by a thunk that logs each invocation and its return value.
// Calls whose return address lies in a DirectPlay module (dplayx's own calls through its vtables) are not logged.
//
// KEY. Every record carries the schema v1 position: the number of main-thread clock calls before it (trPos(), the same
// value trace_rec.js gives its records). Records of other threads (DirectPlay's receive / enumeration threads, the game's
// own network thread) carry the main-thread count at the moment they were written, which depends on real-time scheduling:
// see net_recordings.md section 5. Thread ids are never written: main = 0, other threads 1, 2, ... by first record.
// Nothing host- or boot-dependent is written either: no pointers (objects are ordinals, callbacks call sites, buffers are
// copied with pointers into themselves rewritten as offsets), no thread ids, no time.

